WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.957 --> 00:00:07.322
I have a couple of questions for you—but they’re personal.

2
00:00:07.342 --> 00:00:09.567
So I’m not going to ask for a show of hands.

3
00:00:09.697 --> 00:00:15.287
First, right now, at this stage of your life, are you lonely?

4
00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:19.067
And if not, have you ever been lonely?

5
00:00:19.497 --> 00:00:25.467
My favorite description of loneliness comes from Zoë Heller's book 'Notes on a Scandal.'

6
00:00:25.557 --> 00:00:34.103
And in it, the narrator, who is played in the movie by Judi Dench, distinguishes between passing loneliness and something deeper.

7
00:00:34.123 --> 00:00:38.434
So she observes that people think they know what it's like to be lonely.

8
00:00:38.454 --> 00:00:42.521
You know, they had a bad breakup or a rough time in summer camp.

9
00:00:42.541 --> 00:00:43.827
But then she says this.

10
00:00:44.237 --> 00:00:48.382
About the drip, drip of long-haul, no-end-in-sight solitude, they know nothing.

11
00:00:48.402 --> 00:01:00.181
They don't know what it is to construct an entire weekend around a visit to the launderette, or to sit in a darkened flat on Halloween night, because you can't bear to expose your bleak evening to a crowd of jeering trick-or-treaters.

12
00:01:01.197 --> 00:01:12.692
And she concludes with this: 'I have sat on park benches and trains and school room chairs feeling the great store of unused, objectless love sitting in my belly like a stone until I was sure I would cry out and fall,

13
00:01:12.712 --> 00:01:14.067
flailing to the ground.'

14
00:01:14.297 --> 00:01:16.811
I like this quote for a few reasons.

15
00:01:16.831 --> 00:01:23.067
It talks about solitude, and solitude is a big source of loneliness, but it also talks about love.

16
00:01:23.087 --> 00:01:29.643
You could be with somebody, but if you're not loved, not needed, not respected, you might feel lonely.

17
00:01:29.663 --> 00:01:31.327
And then there's understanding.

18
00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:38.953
Suppose you have something in your head that's important to you, That matters a lot to you, but you feel others don't get it, or don't care to get it, or aren't trying hard enough to get it.

19
00:01:40.599 --> 00:01:42.147
This too could cause loneliness.

20
00:01:42.237 --> 00:01:45.337
Loneliness ravages the body as well as the soul.

21
00:01:45.357 --> 00:01:49.019
A report by the Surgeon General associates it with greater risk of cardiovascular disease, dementia, stroke and the like.

22
00:01:49.039 --> 00:01:53.944
It's the equivalent of smoking 15 cigarettes a day.

23
00:01:57.197 --> 00:02:07.248
Studies in the United States and United Kingdom find that about one in five people describe themselves as often or always lonely.

24
00:02:07.268 --> 00:02:10.527
And the most at-risk group is young adults.

25
00:02:12.157 --> 00:02:17.507
I'm here to talk about a possible cure for loneliness and what it might mean.

26
00:02:17.957 --> 00:02:21.407
And I think we could see loneliness as similar to boredom.

27
00:02:21.637 --> 00:02:26.460
I'm old enough to remember when people were bored all the time.

28
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.946
Long train rides, long car rides, long plane rides.

29
00:02:28.966 --> 00:02:34.767
Late at night when the TV stations switched off and you were all alone, and you had to read.

30
00:02:36.917 --> 00:02:46.759
(Laughter) But now most of us carry around our phones and those phones offer podcasts and movies and books and music and social media and so on.

31
00:02:46.779 --> 00:02:51.167
And so for many of us, boredom is a thing of the past.

32
00:02:51.417 --> 00:02:56.787
Well the cure for loneliness may also be found in our phones and our laptops.

33
00:02:56.807 --> 00:02:59.427
And it is, of course, generative AI.

34
00:02:59.517 --> 00:03:04.784
Last year, some friends and I wrote an article called 'In Praise of Empathic AI.'

35
00:03:04.804 --> 00:03:08.942
If that title makes you cringe and makes you kind of angry, you're not alone.

36
00:03:08.962 --> 00:03:10.315
Our colleagues hated it.

37
00:03:10.503 --> 00:03:13.367
It's the article that I've written that most people hated.

38
00:03:13.937 --> 00:03:14.967
But bear with me.

39
00:03:14.987 --> 00:03:28.502
We started by making a sort of simple point, which is to review a set of studies that have involved — like, you get a whole lot of people interacting with other people, sometimes doctors, sometimes therapists, sometimes just regular people.

40
00:03:28.522 --> 00:03:31.907
And you get a whole lot of people interacting with chatbots.

41
00:03:32.057 --> 00:03:35.855
And then you ask them later: “How did it go?

42
00:03:35.875 --> 00:03:37.000
What’s your experience?”

43
00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:42.950
And people tend to rate the chatbots as more empathic, more caring, more understanding.

44
00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:44.387
And it's no surprise.

45
00:03:44.918 --> 00:03:48.604
AIs are built to be attentive, to be present, to be interested.

46
00:03:48.705 --> 00:03:51.693
An AI is never going to impatiently wait for you to finish telling your story, so it can tell you one of its own.

47
00:03:51.713 --> 00:03:57.364
(Laughter) An AI is never going to say, 'Dude, I'm busy.

48
00:03:57.384 --> 00:03:58.355
Get back to me later.

49
00:03:58.375 --> 00:03:59.367
I got things going on.'

50
00:04:01.061 --> 00:04:09.948
Suppose some future — I don't think it's happening now, but some future AI could serve as a cure for loneliness.

51
00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:11.399
Would this be a good thing?

52
00:04:11.419 --> 00:04:13.279
Well to some extent, yes.

53
00:04:13.299 --> 00:04:15.011
Obviously, yes.

54
00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:27.701
Think about the many millions of elderly people, many in institutions, who go for days and nights without talking to anybody, in perfect agony of isolation.

55
00:04:27.721 --> 00:04:29.325
Many of them have dementia.

56
00:04:29.345 --> 00:04:33.376
Many of them are difficult to interact with for other reasons.

57
00:04:33.396 --> 00:04:36.895
They don't have family and friends to spend time with them.

58
00:04:36.915 --> 00:04:43.392
And there's not enough money in the world to pay people to come and talk to them for hours on end.

59
00:04:43.412 --> 00:04:50.991
If some future version of ChatGPT or Claude or Gemini could ease these people's suffering, it would be a godsend.

60
00:04:51.781 --> 00:04:54.894
But for the rest of us, it's more complicated.

61
00:04:54.914 --> 00:05:00.046
And you can view these cures for loneliness as akin to a powerful painkiller.

62
00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:04.115
Like a powerful painkiller, it can alleviate suffering.

63
00:05:04.135 --> 00:05:09.291
But like a powerful painkiller, it can sometimes come at a terrible cost.

64
00:05:09.381 --> 00:05:14.365
And to make sense of the cost, you have to think about what loneliness is for.

65
00:05:14.385 --> 00:05:24.371
So the neuroscientist John Cacioppo, who founded the modern science of loneliness, argued that it's a biological signal akin to hunger, thirst and pain.

66
00:05:24.641 --> 00:05:27.189
And as such, it does two things for us.

67
00:05:27.209 --> 00:05:34.933
It could sometimes be paralyzing, but when it works out well, it has certain evolved functions that improve our lives.

68
00:05:34.953 --> 00:05:45.291
So one, just as hunger motivates us to seek out food, loneliness and other forms of social suffering motivate us to seek out and maintain social connection.

69
00:05:45.381 --> 00:05:53.934
Because we get lonely, we accept an invitation to a party, we invite a friend out to brunch, we open up the dating apps.

70
00:05:53.954 --> 00:05:56.407
It also helps us regulate relationships.

71
00:05:56.427 --> 00:06:06.591
It helps us try to repair a relationship that has gone wrong, to apologize for messing up, and to try to show genuine interest in our relationship with someone else.

72
00:06:06.961 --> 00:06:11.471
A second thing that loneliness does is help us grow as people.

73
00:06:11.821 --> 00:06:16.831
It's a truism in psychology that we learn from failure.

74
00:06:17.818 --> 00:06:22.031
They call it trial-and-error learning—or reinforcement learning.

75
00:06:22.121 --> 00:06:28.798
The idea is nicely summed up in the movie 'Batman Begins', where young Bruce Wayne’s father asks the future Batman, 'Why do we fall?'

76
00:06:28.818 --> 00:06:32.856
And he answers, 'So we can learn to pick ourselves up.'

77
00:06:34.566 --> 00:06:41.388
This is literally true for toddlers who learn to walk by falling and stumbling and doing things wrong.

78
00:06:41.408 --> 00:06:48.079
It's true for a comedian who learns to improve her act by bombing on stage, by having her jokes fall flat.

79
00:06:48.099 --> 00:06:53.991
It's true for a boxer who learns to evade a punch by getting repeatedly smacked in the face.

80
00:06:54.081 --> 00:06:58.500
Well, failure in the social realm is associated with loneliness.

81
00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:04.151
And can you imagine what it would be like if people never got the signal of failure?

82
00:07:04.361 --> 00:07:13.271
Imagine a teenage boy who never learned to cut short his monologues because his AI companion never got bored.

83
00:07:15.121 --> 00:07:21.191
(Laughter) Imagine an adult who never learned to apologize because her AI companion never needed an apology.

84
00:07:21.481 --> 00:07:26.089
Imagine a world where the answer to the question, 'Am I the asshole?'

85
00:07:26.109 --> 00:07:27.097
(Laughter and Applause) Now you might think this has an easy technological fix.

86
00:07:27.117 --> 00:07:30.011
is always a firm and resounding no.

87
00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:44.062
You could just go into the settings and make it less sycophantic.

88
00:07:44.262 --> 00:07:50.051
And in fact, I don't use AI myself for social purposes, but I use it a lot for my work.

89
00:07:50.181 --> 00:07:53.470
And after a while I got tired of Chat.

90
00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.951
And Claude just said, 'Oh, brilliant question, Paul.

91
00:07:54.971 --> 00:07:56.654
How do you get to be so intelligent?'

92
00:07:56.674 --> 00:07:58.451
Oh, oh, such a clever idea.

93
00:07:59.529 --> 00:08:04.708
So I went into the settings and said, 'Stop.

94
00:08:04.728 --> 00:08:06.032
Do not praise me.

95
00:08:06.052 --> 00:08:06.563
Be blunt.

96
00:08:06.583 --> 00:08:07.622
Just be honest.

97
00:08:07.642 --> 00:08:09.232
Tell it like it is.'

98
00:08:09.252 --> 00:08:10.103
(Laughter and Applause) I kind of missed it. (Laughter) You can call this Cordelia's Problem.

99
00:08:10.123 --> 00:08:12.553
Then a month later, I changed it back.

100
00:08:25.303 --> 00:08:32.902
In Shakespeare's 'King Lear,' the king brings together his three daughters to decide how to divide the kingdom.

101
00:08:32.922 --> 00:08:36.313
He asks each daughter to declare how much she loves him.

102
00:08:36.603 --> 00:08:39.400
The first two daughters lavish praise on him.

103
00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:40.859
They claim to love him infinitely.

104
00:08:40.879 --> 00:08:42.315
He is the center of their lives.

105
00:08:42.335 --> 00:08:43.449
Then Cordelia steps forward.

106
00:08:43.469 --> 00:08:49.048
and says, 'I love you as a daughter should love her father, no more, no less.'

107
00:08:49.068 --> 00:08:53.333
The king, enraged, banishes her from the kingdom.

108
00:08:53.803 --> 00:08:55.933
Now, 'King Lear' is a tragedy.

109
00:08:56.023 --> 00:09:00.953
His two sycophantic daughters did not love him at all and ultimately betray him.

110
00:09:01.123 --> 00:09:05.437
Now some of you are probably thinking, 'Well, I'm not like King Lear.

111
00:09:05.457 --> 00:09:06.553
I welcome pushback.

112
00:09:06.573 --> 00:09:09.573
I don't want that kind of flattery.

113
00:09:09.593 --> 00:09:10.177
And maybe—maybe.

114
00:09:10.197 --> 00:09:16.307
But I suspect some of us prefer the performance of pushback to pushback itself.

115
00:09:16.327 --> 00:09:22.473
So yes, constant affirmation — 'Yes, you're wonderful' — becomes cloying.

116
00:09:22.603 --> 00:09:25.609
But a well-calibrated AI won't do that.

117
00:09:26.081 --> 00:09:29.482
It will resist, argue with you, and push back.

118
00:09:29.502 --> 00:09:33.637
And then finally, in a very satisfying way, it'll say, "I guess you win."

119
00:09:33.657 --> 00:09:35.716
You've totally persuaded me.

120
00:09:35.736 --> 00:09:46.733
And I worry that this sort of intelligent and titrated sycophancy is more corrosive to the soul than a sort of sucking-up yes-men AIs we deal with now.

121
00:09:46.883 --> 00:09:49.813
For some of you, the solution will be clear.

122
00:09:49.903 --> 00:09:56.039
Treat these hypersocial AIs like the powerful painkillers they are.

123
00:09:56.059 --> 00:10:04.073
Make them available to the people who need them the most and forbid their use for the rest of us.

124
00:10:04.343 --> 00:10:07.723
But this is not the solution that I believe in.

125
00:10:07.743 --> 00:10:10.989
I'm not a catastrophist when it comes to AI.

126
00:10:11.009 --> 00:10:19.696
Just like there are people who deal with AI companions in a normal way and benefit from it, and it doesn’t harm their life.

127
00:10:19.984 --> 00:10:37.733
In the same way there are people who resist technological temptations more generally, people who, when faced with the unholy trinity of TikTok, Pornhub and Wordle, nonetheless — (Laughter) — go to the gym, meet with friends, touch grass.

128
00:10:40.023 --> 00:10:45.533
The second reason I don't want to ban these things is more personal.

129
00:10:46.503 --> 00:10:49.513
When I was 11, my mother died of cancer.

130
00:10:50.489 --> 00:11:00.673
I then entered a period of great pain and then problems with social relationships and then a period of loneliness that lasted for years.

131
00:11:01.183 --> 00:11:12.993
I would have benefited so much from someone, or find something, that would listen to me, that would express interest, that I could talk to, that I could share my feelings with.

132
00:11:13.103 --> 00:11:21.633
It seems like a betrayal of my past self to say, 'Tough it out—do without, suffer and learn from your suffering.'

133
00:11:22.323 --> 00:11:34.616
I wonder if the best of all possible worlds is to create imperfect companions, companions that somehow alleviate the pain of loneliness without destroying our humanity.

134
00:11:34.636 --> 00:11:40.697
Companions that are difficult enough to deal with that we learn from interacting with them.

135
00:11:40.717 --> 00:11:48.033
Companions that are sufficiently imperfect that we ultimately abandon them to deal with people.

136
00:11:48.203 --> 00:11:49.219
Now I'm pretty confident we could build such things.

137
00:11:49.239 --> 00:11:53.042
I'm less confident that there's money to be had by doing so.

138
00:11:56.605 --> 00:12:07.275
I accept that it might be a bad business model to create AIs that users ultimately outgrow, but I hope there are people who care enough to do it anyway.

139
00:12:07.365 --> 00:12:08.415
Thank you. (Applause)
